.TH GITLIMES 1 "2026-09-07" "gitlimes 0.1.0" "gitlimes manual"
.SH NAME
gitlimes \- fast, low-memory git history CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gitlimes
.RB [ \-\-color " | " \-\-no\-color ]
.RB [ \-\-pager " | " \-\-no\-pager ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gitlimes
.RB [ \-h " | " \-\-help ]
.br
.B gitlimes
.RB [ \-V " | " \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gitlimes
answers ordinary questions about a git repository \(em what happened, who
did it, how the branches relate \(em and prints a readable, coloured answer
in milliseconds.
It works by spawning
.BR git (1)
and streaming its output through a buffer that is reused for every record.
No command's memory use scales with the length of the repository's history:
.B log
is bounded by one commit record at a time,
.B graph
by the number of branch lanes simultaneously open,
.B who
by the number of distinct authors, and
.B branches
by the number of refs.
A 500,000\-commit repository costs the same as a 50\-commit one.
.PP
.B gitlimes
requires
.B git
on
.IR PATH .
It has no other runtime dependency.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B log
Coloured commit history.
.TP
.B branches
Branch overview with age, tracking status and staleness.
.TP
.B who
Author and contribution statistics.
.TP
.B graph
Unicode branch graph.
.PP
Every command accepts
.B \-h ,
.BR \-\-help ", and " \-\-json .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These may appear before or after the command name, but never after a bare
.B \-\-
that introduces revisions or paths \(em an operand spelled
.B \-\-color
is then left alone, not consumed as a flag.
.TP
.BR \-\-color ", " \-\-no\-color
Force colour on or off, overriding both terminal detection and
.BR NO_COLOR .
.TP
.BR \-\-pager ", " \-\-no\-pager
Force paging on or off, overriding terminal detection.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage for
.B gitlimes
itself, or for the command it follows.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH LOG
.B gitlimes log
.RI [ rev ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-author
.IR pattern ]
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR date ]
.RB [ \-\-until
.IR date ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-oneline ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
[\fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR ...]
.PP
Prints commit history: short hash, relative age, author, ref decorations
(local branches green, remote branches red, tags yellow, the checked\-out
ref in bold), and subject.
A revision may be given to start from somewhere other than
.BR HEAD .
Everything after a literal
.B \-\-
is passed to git as a path filter, so a path that happens to look like a
flag is never misread as one.
.TP
.BI "\-n, \-\-max\-count " N
Show at most
.I N
commits. Passed straight through to git, so memory does not depend on
.IR N .
.TP
.BI "\-\-author " pattern
Only commits whose author matches
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI "\-\-since, \-\-until " date
Only commits in the given date range. Accepts anything
.B git log
does, including relative forms like
.RB \(dq "2 weeks ago" \(dq.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Include commits reachable from any ref, not just
.BR HEAD .
.TP
.B \-\-oneline
Print only the hash and subject.
.TP
.B \-\-json
See
.B JSON OUTPUT
below.
.SH BRANCHES
.B gitlimes branches
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-\-stale
.IR days ]
.RB [ \-\-vs
.IR ref ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Lists branches sorted by last commit, with age, author and upstream
tracking. The checked\-out branch is marked with
.BR * .
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Also list remote\-tracking branches.
.TP
.BI "\-\-stale " days
Mark branches whose last commit is older than
.I days
days.
.I days
must be a non\-negative whole number; a value that could put the cutoff in
the future or overflow the calculation is rejected rather than silently
misinterpreted.
.TP
.BI "\-\-vs " ref
Show ahead/behind against
.I ref
instead of each branch's own upstream.
On git 2.41 and later this costs a single extra git process no matter how
many branches are listed, using
.BR for\-each\-ref (1)'s
.BR %(ahead\-behind:) ;
older git falls back to one process per branch.
.TP
.B \-\-json
See
.B JSON OUTPUT
below.
.SH WHO
.B gitlimes who
.RI [ path ]
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR date ]
.RB [ \-\-limit
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-lines ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Lists authors by commit count, with their share of the total, when they
were first and last seen, and an activity sparkline. Authors are grouped
by email address (case\-insensitively), so one person committing under
several display names is still counted once; the email column shows which
address is meant.
.PP
The sparkline spans the whole history shown rather than a fixed recent
window, so a repository that has been quiet for years still shows a
shape instead of a row of blanks. Its header states what one block is
worth \(em from a day up to a year or more \(em because the scale adapts
to the oldest commit found and is never left implicit.
.TP
.RI [ path ]
Restrict to commits touching
.IR path ,
to answer "who owns this file or directory".
.TP
.BI "\-\-since " date
Only count commits more recent than
.IR date .
.TP
.BI "\-\-limit " N
Show only the top
.I N
authors by commit count.
.TP
.B \-\-lines
Also total added and removed lines per author. Roughly doubles the work
git has to do, which is why it is opt\-in.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Include commits reachable from any ref, not just
.BR HEAD .
.TP
.B \-\-json
See
.B JSON OUTPUT
below.
.SH GRAPH
.B gitlimes graph
.RI [ rev ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-\-ascii ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-author
.IR pattern ]
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR date ]
[\fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR ...]
.PP
Draws the branch graph using Unicode box\-drawing characters. A filled
dot is an ordinary commit; a hollow dot is a merge. Each branch keeps one
colour for its entire life, even as it changes column, and lane
compaction keeps the drawing as narrow as the true concurrency allows.
Below the eviction limit described under
.BR "LIMITS" ,
every parent\-child edge git reports is drawn, correctly and
unambiguously \(em no edge is lost or invented. The exact column layout
is not guaranteed to match
.BR "git log \-\-graph" :
a new lane always takes the leftmost free column and two lanes fold
together only once their shared commit is reached, so some multi\-branch
histories place a lane, or close one, on a different row than git would.
The graph itself is always correct; the picture of it is usually more
compact \(em a crossing merge that needs three rows in git's ASCII form
often needs one here \(em but occasionally the reverse.
.TP
.BI "\-n, \-\-max\-count " N
Show at most
.I N
commits (default 40).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Include all refs, not just
.BR HEAD .
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
Draw with plain ASCII
.RB ( * ", " | ", " / ", " \e )
instead of box\-drawing characters, for terminals or fonts that lack them.
.TP
.BI "\-\-author " pattern
.TP
.BI "\-\-since " date
As in
.BR log .
.TP
.B \-\-json
See
.B JSON OUTPUT
below; each object additionally carries a
.B graph
field with that commit's lane geometry
.RI ( col ", " lanes ", " merge ", " closing ", " opening ", " shifts ),
so a different renderer \(em an SVG export, a canvas, a TUI \(em can draw
the same graph without recomputing the layout.
.SH LIMITS
A branch lane is normally retired the moment the commit it is waiting for
is reached. That assumption does not hold when git is not shown the whole
graph \(em under
.BR \-\-author ,
.BR \-\-since ,
a path filter, or a shallow clone \(em because a commit's parents are
still the real ones even when the filter hides them, so a lane can be
left waiting for a commit that will never arrive. To keep memory bounded
regardless, the number of simultaneously open lanes is capped at 64; past
that point the lane that has gone longest without a commit is evicted,
which drops one edge from the drawing. A single merge with more than 64
parents is not affected by this: the table grows just enough to hold that
one commit's own parents, since that is bounded by the commit and not by
the length of the history.
.PP
When an edge is dropped this way,
.B gitlimes
prints a warning to standard error rather than presenting the drawing as
exact. In ordinary use \(em an unfiltered graph, or a filtered one on a
repository with a normal number of active branches \(em the cap is never
reached.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
Every command accepts
.B \-\-json ,
which emits newline\-delimited JSON (NDJSON): one self\-contained object
per line, never a wrapping array. This keeps output streamable \(em safe
to pipe into
.BR head (1),
and never invalid mid\-stream the way a JSON array would be while it is
still being written. Pipe into
.B jq \-s
to collect it into an array.
.PP
There is deliberately no version field in the output. Existing keys are
not renamed, retyped, or removed without a note in the project's
.B CHANGELOG.md ;
new keys may appear in any release, so a consumer should ignore keys it
does not recognise. Some keys are present only when they apply \(em
.B head
on a commit,
.B track
on a branch,
.B added
and
.B removed
on an author only with
.BR \-\-lines .
.B gitlimes \-\-version
identifies which release produced a given line.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to any non\-empty value, disables colour, matching the
convention at
.BR no\-color.org .
An empty value
.RI ( NO_COLOR= )
is treated as unset, so it can be used to cancel an inherited setting.
Overridden by
.B \-\-color
and
.BR \-\-no\-color .
.TP
.BR GITLIMES_PAGER ", " PAGER
Set the pager used when output is paged, checked in that order.
.B GITLIMES_PAGER
lets the pager for
.B gitlimes
be chosen independently of the pager used elsewhere. An empty value
disables paging, matching how
.BR git (1)
and most other tools treat an empty
.BR PAGER .
The value may be a bare command
.RB ( "less \-S" ),
a path that contains spaces, quoted or not
.RB ( "C:\eProgram Files\eGit\eusr\ebin\eless.exe \-S" ),
or nothing at all, in which case
.B less
is used if it can be found. The pager is started only once there is
output to send it, so a command that fails before producing any never
opens one over the top of git's own error message.
.TP
.B PATH
.B git
must be found on
.BR PATH .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed: an unknown flag, a flag missing
its required value, or a value that failed to parse.
.TP
.B other
Any other non\-zero code is
.BR git 's
own exit status for the underlying command, propagated unchanged \(em
.B 128
for "not a git repository" and similar fatal errors, for instance.
.B gitlimes
adds nothing of its own in this case, since git has already explained
itself on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B gitlimes log \-n 20
The last 20 commits on the current branch.
.TP
.B gitlimes log \-\-author=Jane \-\-since="2 weeks ago"
What Jane has committed in the last two weeks.
.TP
.B gitlimes branches \-a \-\-stale 90
Every branch, local and remote, with anything untouched for 90 days
flagged.
.TP
.B gitlimes branches \-\-vs main
Every branch's ahead/behind count against
.BR main ,
in one git process.
.TP
.B gitlimes who \-\-lines src/
Who has touched
.B src/
and how many lines they have added or removed there.
.TP
.B gitlimes graph \-\-all \-n 60
The last 60 commits across every branch, drawn as a graph.
.TP
.B gitlimes log \-\-json | jq \-r .subject
The subject line of every commit, extracted with
.BR jq (1).
.TP
.B GITLIMES_PAGER=\(dqless \-S\(dq gitlimes log
Page the log through
.B less
with long lines truncated instead of wrapped.
.SH FILES
.B gitlimes
reads no configuration file of its own; every setting is a command\-line
flag or one of the environment variables listed above.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git\-log (1),
.BR git\-for\-each\-ref (1),
.BR jq (1)
.PP
The full project documentation, including the JSON schema and the case
for its design choices, is in
.B README.md
in the project repository:
.br
